from checkpoint_support import *

def bridge(id, stem, options, feedback, error, *checks):
    task(id,stem,options,feedback,'application',error,proof(*checks) if checks else None)
    patch(id,'Repair skill retained; bridge adds one bounded application, comparison or inference.',canonicalDifficulty='medium')

bridge('43139','A report defines its ratio as debt held by the public divided by annual GDP. GDP is $30 trillion and that debt is $30.3 trillion; gross federal debt is $36 trillion. Which ratio belongs in this report?', ['101%, using debt held by the public','120%, using gross federal debt','99%, dividing GDP by public debt','20%, using the difference between the debt measures'], '30.3/30×100=101%. The named numerator excludes the extra intragovernmental holdings included in gross debt.','Substitutes gross debt for debt held by the public.',('30.3/30*100',101))
bridge('43160','A larger deficit reduces public saving while private saving is unchanged. In a closed-economy loanable-funds model with unchanged investment demand, what pressure does this place on interest and private investment?', ['Interest rises and private investment falls','Interest falls and private investment rises','Interest rises and private investment rises','Interest falls and private investment falls'], 'Lower national saving shifts loanable-funds supply left. The higher equilibrium interest rate reduces investment demanded.','Treats reduced public saving as greater loanable-funds supply.')
bridge('43234','Households save more at each interest rate. In the loanable-funds market, with investment demand unchanged, which equilibrium change follows?', ['Supply shifts right, lowering interest and raising investment','Demand shifts right, raising interest and investment','Supply shifts left, raising interest and lowering investment','Demand shifts left, lowering interest and investment'], 'Saving supplies loanable funds. A rightward supply shift lowers their price (interest) and raises the equilibrium quantity invested.','Places household saving on the investment-demand curve.')
bridge('ECON-NL-CATCH-UP-EFFECT-6024','Two countries share technology and worker skills, but A has much less capital per worker. Both add the same small amount of capital per worker. With diminishing marginal returns, which comparison follows?', ['A gains more output per worker from that addition','B gains more because its starting output is higher','Both gain the same because additions are equal','A loses output because it began with less capital'], 'Under comparable other conditions, capital is more productive at the margin where it is scarcer. This is conditional catch-up potential.','Confuses a higher starting level with a higher marginal return.')
bridge('ECON-NL-DIMINISHING-RETURNS-6023','With labor and technology fixed, successive identical machines raise output by12,9 and6 units. A plan assumes a fourth adds12. Which revision fits diminishing marginal returns?', ['Expect a gain smaller than6 if the pattern continues','Expect a gain of12 because the machines are identical','Expect total output to fall by6','Expect every previous machine’s output to disappear'], 'Diminishing marginal returns concern successively smaller additions to output, not a fall in total output or identical gains from identical inputs.','Confuses identical physical inputs with equal marginal products.')
bridge('ECON-NL-HUMAN-CAPITAL-6020','A factory buys machines but cannot operate them because workers lack the necessary training. Which complementary change would make the physical-capital investment usable?', ['Train workers to operate the new machines','Replace worker training with more idle machines','Count the machines as natural resources','Raise product prices without changing production'], 'Training supplies complementary human capital, allowing the new physical capital to contribute to output.','Treats physical capital as productive without complementary worker skills.')
bridge('ECON-NL-NATURAL-RESOURCES-6021','A mineral deposit is discovered, but output cannot rise until transport links reach it. Which conclusion applies the natural-resource distinction?', ['Resource availability improved, but complementary infrastructure is needed to use it','Current productivity must already have risen by the full value of the deposit','Only human capital changed because discovery involves knowledge','The deposit is produced capital because it has economic value'], 'The natural resource exists, but access requires produced infrastructure. A resource discovery is not itself measured production or guaranteed immediate productivity.','Equates resource discovery with immediate usable output.')
bridge('ECON-NL-TECHNOLOGICAL-KNOWLEDGE-6022','A new production method raises output with the same workforce and machines. If the method is shared with a comparable second plant, what change could occur without more physical capital?', ['Its output per worker could rise','Only its number of machines could rise','Its output per worker must fall','Only its nominal selling price could change'], 'Using a better method can raise productivity at unchanged inputs; technology is distinct from adding machines.','Attributes every productivity gain to more physical capital.')
bridge('ECON-NL-GROWTH-POLICY-6025','A country has available technology and willing investors, but contracts are rarely enforced. Which policy directly addresses this stated obstacle to productive investment?', ['Improve reliable contract enforcement','Raise aggregate demand without changing enforcement','Restrict worker training while subsidizing consumption','Reclassify transfers as investment in the accounts'], 'Reliable enforcement lets investors expect to retain contractual returns. This addresses the specified incentive constraint rather than merely adding short-run spending.','Applies demand stimulus to an institutional investment constraint.')
bridge('ECON-NL-PRODUCTIVITY-CALCULATION-6018','A team must produce2,400 units. Productivity improves from8 to10 units per hour. How many labor hours are now needed?', ['240 hours','300 hours','24,000 hours','192 hours'], 'Required hours=output/productivity=2400/10=240, down from300.','Multiplies output by productivity instead of dividing.',('2400/10',240))
bridge('ECON-NL-REAL-GDP-PER-PERSON-6017','Real GDP is $3 trillion and population100 million. If population rises to120 million with real GDP unchanged, what is new real GDP per person?', ['$25,000','$30,000','$36,000','$2,500'], 'Divide3 trillion by120 million to get25,000. More people share unchanged real output.','Holds per-person GDP fixed when population changes.',('3e12/120e6',25000))
bridge('ECON-NL-RULE-OF-70-6016','Using the Rule of70, compare a2% annual growth rate with4%. What happens to approximate doubling time?', ['It falls from35 to17.5 years','It rises from17.5 to35 years','It falls from70 to35 years','It stays35 years because both rates are positive'], '70/2=35;70/4=17.5. Doubling the growth rate halves approximate doubling time.','Makes doubling time proportional to the growth rate.',('70/2',35),('70/4',17.5))
bridge('ECON-NL-INCOME-EXPENDITURE-IDENTITY-6000','Final domestic output sells for$210. Production income records show wages$140 and rent/interest$30. With no taxes, depreciation or discrepancy, what profit completes the income ledger?', ['$40','$70','$180','$210'], 'All income from producing the output sums to210. The missing profit is210−140−30=40.','Adds spending to income instead of reconciling the two ledgers.',('210-140-30',40))
bridge('ECON-NL-INTERMEDIATE-VS-FINAL-GOODS-6002','A mill sells flour for$50 and a bakery sells the bread made from it to households for$120. The bakery’s value added is$70. Which GDP total agrees under both final-sales and value-added accounting?', ['$120','$170','$70','$240'], 'Final bread sales120 equal mill value added50 plus bakery value added70. Counting flour again with final sales double-counts it.','Counts intermediate sales again alongside final goods.',('50+70',120))
bridge('ECON-NL-GDP-COMPONENTS-IDENTITY-6003','A household buys a newly produced domestic car for$30,000; a firm buys a newly produced domestic machine for$20,000. Which pair enters the expenditure accounts?', ['C=$30,000; I=$20,000','C=$50,000; I=$0','C=$0; I=$50,000','C=$20,000; I=$30,000'], 'Household final purchases enter consumption; productive equipment purchased by a firm enters investment.','Classifies durable household consumption as business investment.')
bridge('ECON-NL-INVENTORY-INVESTMENT-6005','A factory makes$100 of new domestic output and sells$80 this period. The unsold$20 stays in inventory. What current production total is recorded?', ['$100: sales80 plus inventory investment20','$80: only sold goods count','$120: production100 plus inventory20','$20: only inventory is new production'], 'Unsold current production is inventory investment. Final sales80 plus inventory accumulation20 record the same100 produced.','Excludes unsold current production or counts it twice.',('80+20',100))
bridge('ECON-NL-GDP-DEFLATOR-6007','Nominal GDP is$180 and the GDP deflator is120 (base100). What real GDP value makes the output comparison use base-year prices?', ['$150','$216','$180','$60'], 'Real GDP=nominal GDP/(deflator/100)=180/1.2=150.','Multiplies nominal GDP by the price index instead of deflating.',('180/1.2',150))
bridge('ECON-NL-NOMINAL-VS-REAL-GDP-6006','Real GDP per person rises, while pollution rises and leisure falls. Does the GDP gain alone establish that overall well-being improved?', ['No; those omitted dimensions could offset the measured output gain','Yes; real GDP directly includes pollution damage and leisure','No; real GDP must fall whenever leisure falls','Yes; per-person GDP measures the distribution and value of all nonmarket activity'], 'The measured output gain is real, but GDP does not settle the valuation of lost leisure and environmental quality.','Treats real GDP per person as a complete welfare measure.')
bridge('ECON-NL-CPI-CALCULATION-6009','A fixed basket costs$400 in the base year. If today’s CPI is125 (base100), what is today’s basket cost?', ['$500','$320','$525','$125'], 'Current cost=400×125/100=500.','Reverses the price-index ratio.',('400*125/100',500))
bridge('ECON-NL-INFLATION-RATE-6010','CPI rises from125 to150. A rent of$1,000 is adjusted by that inflation rate. What is the new rent?', ['$1,200','$1,250','$1,500','$1,167'], 'Inflation=(150−125)/125=20%;1000×1.20=1200.','Treats the index-point change as the percentage inflation rate.',('(150-125)/125*100',20),('1000*150/125',1200))
bridge('ECON-NL-INFLATION-ADJUSTMENT-6014','A salary was$40,000 at CPI100. At CPI160 it is$60,000. How much additional salary would preserve the original purchasing power?', ['$4,000','$24,000','$20,000','$0'], 'The indexed target is40000×160/100=64000; the current60000 falls4000 short.','Compares nominal salaries without applying the CPI ratio.',('40000*1.6-60000',4000))
bridge('ECON-NL-REAL-INTEREST-RATE-6015','Use the Fisher approximation. A loan fixes nominal interest at9%. Expected inflation was5%, but actual inflation is7%. What realized real rate replaces the expected4%?', ['2%','4%','7%','16%'], 'Realized real interest≈9−7=2%; actual, rather than expected, inflation determines realized purchasing power.','Uses expected inflation for a realized real return.',('9-7',2))
bridge('ECON-NL-SUBSTITUTION-BIAS-6011','Beef becomes dearer relative to chicken. Households substitute chicken, but a fixed CPI basket keeps the old beef quantity. Relative to the cost of the adjusted purchasing pattern, what bias can result?', ['The fixed basket overstates the cost increase','The fixed basket understates the cost increase','The basket automatically records the new chicken quantity','The basket measures only chicken inflation'], 'The fixed basket misses the saving available from substitution toward relatively cheaper chicken.','Assumes a fixed basket automatically incorporates consumer substitution.')
bridge('ECON-NL-LABOR-FORCE-CALCULATION-6026','There are90 employed and10 unemployed. Four unemployed people stop searching; nobody else changes status. What is the new labor force?', ['96','100','94','90'], 'The four move from unemployed to outside the labor force.90+6=96; employment stays90.','Keeps discouraged nonsearchers in the labor force.',('90+10-4',96))
bridge('ECON-NL-UNEMPLOYMENT-RATE-6027','There are152 employed and8 unemployed. Two unemployed workers find jobs. What is the new unemployment rate?', ['3.75%','5%','3.9%','6.25%'], 'The labor force stays160 while unemployment becomes6:6/160=3.75%.','Reduces the labor-force denominator when an unemployed worker becomes employed.',('6/160*100',3.75))
bridge('ECON-NL-LABOR-FORCE-PARTICIPATION-6028','Adult population is250 and the labor force150. Ten previously nonparticipating adults start active job search without finding jobs. What is the new LFPR?', ['64%','60%','56%','40%'], 'Job seekers join the labor force:160/250=64%. They need not already be employed to participate.','Excludes active unemployed job seekers from participation.',('160/250*100',64))
bridge('ECON-NL-EMPLOYMENT-CLASSIFICATION-6029','A survey has90 employed and10 unemployed. One employed person cuts paid work to one hour while seeking full-time work. How do measured employment and unemployment change?', ['Neither changes; the person is still employed','Employment falls1 and unemployment rises1','Employment falls1 and the labor force falls1','Unemployment falls1 and employment rises1'], 'One paid hour still counts as employed under the stated status rule. Underemployment changes without changing these measured counts.','Classifies an employed part-time worker as unemployed.')
bridge('ECON-NL-CYCLICAL-UNEMPLOYMENT-6033','Sales fall economy-wide during recession and trained workers are laid off. If demand returns with skills still suitable, which unemployment component should most directly shrink?', ['Cyclical unemployment','Structural unemployment from obsolete skills','Frictional unemployment from voluntary job changes','Natural unemployment caused by permanent skill mismatch'], 'The unemployment arose from weak aggregate demand rather than an enduring mismatch; recovery can reduce its cyclical component.','Attributes recession layoffs to a permanent skill mismatch.')
bridge('ECON-NL-FRICTIONAL-UNEMPLOYMENT-6031','Two workers are unemployed: A is briefly between suitable jobs; B’s old occupation has disappeared and employers require skills B lacks. Which worker is more directly helped by retraining?', ['B, because the problem is structural mismatch','A, because all job search is structural mismatch','Both equally, because duration alone defines unemployment type','Neither, because training can affect only cyclical unemployment'], 'B lacks the skills demanded in available jobs. A’s short search between suitable jobs is frictional; faster matching may help without retraining.','Confuses job matching with structural skill mismatch.')
bridge('ECON-NL-STRUCTURAL-UNEMPLOYMENT-6032','Automation removes jobs whose workers lack skills for new vacancies. A policy only boosts demand for output without changing skills. Which limit follows?', ['The skill mismatch may persist despite stronger demand','All mismatch disappears as soon as spending increases','Unemployment becomes frictional solely because demand rises','The workers leave the labor force automatically'], 'More demand need not give displaced workers the skills needed for existing vacancies. A structural problem can require retraining or mobility.','Assumes demand expansion directly removes a skill mismatch.')
bridge('ECON-NL-UNIONS-EFFICIENCY-WAGES-6035','A firm pays above-market wages to reduce quits. Its wage bill rises$10,000 but recruiting and training costs fall$15,000. What net cost implication supports the efficiency-wage rationale?', ['Relevant costs fall$5,000 despite higher wages','Relevant costs rise$25,000','Relevant costs rise$10,000 because turnover savings do not count','Relevant costs fall$15,000 with no wage cost'], 'Combine the wage increase and turnover saving:10000−15000=−5000. Higher wages can reduce other costs.','Evaluates the wage increase without its turnover-cost offset.',('10000-15000',-5000))

bridge('ECON-SP-CALCULATE-REQUIRED-RESERVES-6004','In a hypothetical system with a12.5% reserve requirement, a bank has$8,000 deposits and$1,200 reserves. How much exceeds required reserves?', ['$200','$1,000','$1,200','$800'], 'Required reserves=.125×8000=1000; excess reserves=1200−1000=200.','Treats total reserves as excess reserves.',('1200-.125*8000',200))
bridge('LG-B-6003','In a hypothetical system with a12% reserve requirement, a bank has$900,000 deposits and$100,000 reserves. What reserve adjustment meets the requirement?', ['Obtain$8,000 additional reserves','Lend$8,000 of excess reserves','Obtain$108,000 additional reserves','Lend$100,000 because all reserves are excess'], 'Required reserves=.12×900000=108000. Existing reserves100000 leave a shortfall8000.','Confuses a reserve shortfall with lending capacity.',('.12*900000-100000',8000))
bridge('LG-B-6005','In a hypothetical system initially at required reserves, a$10,000 currency deposit enters with rr=20%. Banks lend all excess reserves and all payments are redeposited, with no other constraints. Total deposits can rise$50,000 including the initial deposit. What is the net increase in currency-plus-checking money?', ['$40,000','$50,000','$10,000','$60,000'], 'Deposits rise50000 but currency held by the public falls10000. Net currency-plus-checking money rises40000.','Counts the initial currency-to-deposit conversion as net new money.',('50000-10000',40000))
bridge('ECON-SP-CENTRAL-BANK-ROLE-6002','A solvent bank faces a temporary withdrawal surge and cannot quickly sell assets. Which central-bank function could address the liquidity problem?', ['Lender-of-last-resort lending against acceptable collateral','Directly setting household income-tax rates','Making the bank’s owners immune from all investment losses','Recording the bank’s withdrawals as government purchases'], 'Emergency liquidity lending can help a solvent bank meet withdrawals. It is distinct from tax policy or guaranteeing shareholders against losses.','Confuses temporary liquidity support with fiscal authority or capital guarantees.')
bridge('LG-B-6002','Officials propose changing the policy interest-rate setting, while legislators propose a purchases increase. Which institution-policy pairing is appropriate?', ['Central bank for the rate decision; fiscal authorities for purchases','Central bank for both because all stabilization is monetary','Legislators for both because rates are a spending item','Fiscal authorities for rates; central bank for public purchases'], 'Interest-rate policy is monetary; government spending is fiscal. The implementation authority follows the instrument used.','Confuses monetary and fiscal policy authority.')
bridge('ECON-SP-LIMITS-OF-MONETARY-CONTROL-6008','A reserve injection could support$1,000 of deposits in the simple maximum model. Banks instead retain additional excess reserves. Relative to that maximum, what should an analyst forecast?', ['Less deposit creation, other conditions equal','More deposit creation because excess reserves are loans','Exactly$1,000 regardless of lending decisions','No reserves remaining because deposits remove reserves from the system'], 'Additional reserve retention leaves less available for repeated lending and deposit expansion than the restrictive simple maximum assumes.','Treats the simple multiplier maximum as guaranteed actual expansion.')
bridge('LG-B-6008','A$100 reserve injection is followed by$400 in additional deposits. The simple model with rr=10% predicts$1,000. Which observed multiplier should be used to describe this episode?', ['4, below the simple maximum10','10, because the legal ratio fixes actual expansion','0.25, because reserves are divided by deposits','40, because both quantities must be expressed as percentages'], 'Observed deposit expansion per reserve dollar=400/100=4. Leakages or other lending constraints can leave it below10.','Substitutes the simple theoretical multiplier for the observed ratio.',('400/100',4))
bridge('ECON-SP-VALUE-OF-MONEY-6012','The price level rises from2 to5. Using value of money=1/P, how many units of the reference basket can$10 buy afterward?', ['2 units','5 units','20 units','50 units'], 'At P=5 each dollar buys1/5 basket unit;10/5=2 units.','Multiplies cash by the price level to measure purchasing power.',('10/5',2))
bridge('LG-B-6009','Money’s value falls from1/2 to1/4 basket unit per dollar. What happens to the dollars needed to buy one unchanged basket?', ['They rise from$2 to$4','They fall from$4 to$2','They rise from$0.50 to$0.75','They stay$2 because the basket is unchanged'], 'The basket price is the reciprocal of money’s value:2 then4.','Uses the value-of-money fraction directly as the basket price.')
bridge('LG-B-6010','Use the quantity-theory growth approximation. Money grows12% and output4%. If velocity now falls2% rather than staying stable, what inflation rate is predicted?', ['6%','8%','10%','18%'], 'Inflation≈gM+gV−gY=12−2−4=6%. The velocity decline lowers spending growth.','Ignores a stated change in velocity.',('12-2-4',6))
bridge('ECON-SP-MONETARY-NEUTRALITY-6014','All nominal prices double in a long-run neutrality comparison. A worker’s nominal wage also doubles from$20 to$40. What happens to the real wage?', ['It is unchanged','It doubles','It halves','It rises by$20 of purchasing power'], 'Real wage=W/P. Doubling numerator and denominator leaves the ratio unchanged.','Treats a nominal wage rise as a real gain when prices rise proportionately.')
bridge('LG-B-6012','In a long-run neutrality comparison money, nominal GDP and the price level all double. What happens to real GDP=nominal GDP/(price index/100)?', ['It remains unchanged','It doubles','It quadruples','It falls by half'], 'Proportional nominal GDP and price increases cancel in the real-output ratio.','Counts a proportional price increase as real output growth.')
bridge('ECON-SP-FISHER-EFFECT-6015','Using the Fisher approximation, expected inflation rises from6% to8% while the expected real rate remains2%. What new nominal rate is consistent with both conditions?', ['10%','8%','6%','4%'], 'Nominal≈real+expected inflation=2+8=10%, up from8%.','Holds the nominal rate fixed despite higher expected inflation and a fixed real rate.',('2+8',10))
bridge('LG-B-6013','A contract quotes8% nominal interest and expected inflation6%. If a new contract has expected inflation3% with the same expected real return, what nominal rate should it quote?', ['5%','8%','11%','3%'], 'Original expected real return≈8−6=2%; new nominal≈2+3=5%.','Changes nominal and expected inflation in opposite directions at a fixed real rate.',('8-6+3',5))
bridge('ECON-SP-INFLATION-TAX-6018','A household holds$100 cash as prices rise10%. It receives no interest. How does this illustrate the inflation tax on money holders?', ['The same cash buys less, about$90.91 in initial-price purchasing power','The cash balance automatically rises to$110','Real purchasing power rises10% because nominal cash is unchanged','The household owes a separate$10 statutory income-tax bill'], 'Real cash purchasing power is100/1.10≈90.91. The loss need not appear as a separate tax bill.','Treats inflation tax as an explicit statutory tax payment.',('100/1.1',90.909090909))
bridge('ECON-SP-MENU-COSTS-6017','Inflation leads a shop to reprint signs at$200 per update. Updates rise from2 to6 per year. What additional annual resource cost is a menu cost?', ['$800','$1,200','$400','$200'], 'Four additional updates cost4×200=800. The cost is changing posted prices, not the price increase itself.','Confuses total repricing costs with the added cost requested.',('(6-2)*200',800))
bridge('ECON-SP-SHOELEATHER-COSTS-6016','To avoid holding cash during inflation, a household makes four extra bank trips per month, each taking30 minutes. What monthly time cost illustrates shoeleather costs?', ['2 hours','4 hours','0.5 hour','120 hours'], 'Four trips×30 minutes=120 minutes=2 hours spent managing balances.','Counts only one trip or confuses minutes with hours.',('4*30/60',2))
bridge('LG-B-6014','Use the Fisher approximation. A fixed nominal loan pays9%; expected inflation was3%, actual inflation10%. Compared with the expected6% real return, who benefits from the surprise?', ['The borrower; realized real interest is about−1%','The lender; realized real interest is about19%','The borrower; realized real interest remains6%','Neither; actual inflation cannot change a fixed nominal contract’s real value'], 'Realized return≈9−10=−1%, seven points below the expected6%. The borrower repays less purchasing power than anticipated.','Uses expected rather than actual inflation to measure realized return.',('9-10',-1))
bridge('LG-B-6015','A store spends$600 updating prices while a household loses three hours making extra cash-management trips. Which comparison identifies the two resource costs?', ['The store incurs menu costs; the household incurs shoeleather costs','The store incurs shoeleather costs; the household incurs menu costs','Both are transfers from lenders to borrowers','Both are changes in real GDP caused solely by counting cash'], 'Repricing resources are menu costs; extra balance-management effort is shoeleather cost. Both use resources beyond a mere transfer between borrowers and lenders.','Reverses repricing and cash-management cost categories.')

bridge('ECON-SP-EXPLAIN-AD-SLOPE-6019','With nominal money supply fixed, the price level falls. Apply the interest-rate channel of the AD slope: what happens to money demand, interest and investment?', ['Money demand falls, interest falls, investment rises','Money demand rises, interest rises, investment falls','Money supply rises, interest rises, investment rises','Money demand falls, interest rises, investment falls'], 'Lower transaction-price needs reduce nominal money demand, lowering the interest rate and encouraging investment. The money supply was held fixed.','Reverses the money-demand response to a lower price level.')
bridge('ECON-SP-IDENTIFY-AD-SHIFTERS-6020','Business investment falls for reasons unrelated to the price level. With upward-sloping SRAS unchanged, what short-run output/price combination follows from the AD shift?', ['Both output and the price level fall','Output rises while the price level falls','Output falls while the price level rises','Both output and the price level rise'], 'Lower autonomous investment shifts AD left; along unchanged SRAS the new equilibrium has lower output and prices.','Mistakes an adverse demand shift for an adverse supply shift.')
bridge('ECON-SP-IDENTIFY-SRAS-SHIFTERS-6023','Oil costs fall while aggregate demand is unchanged. What output/price combination follows from the resulting SRAS shift?', ['Output rises and the price level falls','Both output and the price level rise','Output falls and the price level rises','Both output and the price level fall'], 'Lower input costs shift SRAS right, increasing output and reducing the price level at the new demand intersection.','Uses demand-shift price/output signs for a supply improvement.')
bridge('ECON-SP-EXPECTED-PRICE-SRAS-SHIFT-6024','Expected prices rise and new wage contracts raise nominal costs. With aggregate demand fixed, apply the SRAS shift to output and the price level.', ['Output falls and the price level rises','Output and the price level both rise','Output rises and the price level falls','Output and the price level both fall'], 'Higher expected wages/costs shift SRAS left. The intersection with unchanged AD has lower output and higher prices.','Treats higher expected costs as an increase in short-run supply.')
bridge('ECON-SP-IDENTIFY-LRAS-6021','Technology raises potential output from100 to110. If actual output stays100, what output gap exists relative to the new LRAS?', ['A10-unit recessionary gap','A10-unit inflationary gap','No gap because actual output did not change','A110-unit recessionary gap'], 'The benchmark rose:actual100−potential110=−10. A capacity gain can open a shortfall if demand does not keep pace.','Uses the old potential-output benchmark after a capacity change.',('100-110',-10))
bridge('ECON-SP-CLASSIFY-OUTPUT-GAP-6027','Actual output is$19 trillion and potential$20 trillion. If demand increases output by$0.5 trillion without changing capacity, what gap remains?', ['A$0.5 trillion recessionary gap','A$0.5 trillion inflationary gap','A$1.5 trillion recessionary gap','No gap because output rose'], 'New actual output19.5 remains0.5 below potential20.','Confuses a partial recovery with complete gap closure.',('20-(19+.5)',.5))
bridge('ECON-SP-EXPLAIN-LONG-RUN-ADJUSTMENT-6028','Output is above potential, but wage adjustment is slow. A proposed demand stimulus would arrive before self-correction. How would that stimulus initially affect the existing gap, other conditions equal?', ['It would enlarge the positive output gap','It would directly lower expected wages and close the gap','It would reduce aggregate demand and close the gap','It would permanently raise LRAS by the size of the stimulus'], 'Extra demand at an already above-potential starting point pushes activity further above capacity before wage adjustment.','Applies recession stimulus to an inflationary output gap.')
bridge('ECON-SP-CHOOSE-STABILIZATION-RESPONSE-6054','After a demand recession, falling wages are already shifting SRAS right toward recovery. A stimulus takes a year to arrive. What fact is most relevant to whether it will stabilize output at arrival?', ['The expected remaining gap when the stimulus arrives','Only the size of the gap when the recession began','Whether the original shock was measured in dollars','Whether the economy has ever used fiscal policy before'], 'Compare the intervention with conditions at implementation. Existing self-correction can reduce or eliminate the gap before delayed stimulus arrives.','Targets a historical gap without allowing for recovery during the policy lag.')
bridge('ECON-SP-TRACE-SUPPLY-SHOCK-6026','An oil-price spike shifts SRAS left. Authorities then expand demand to support output. Relative to leaving demand fixed, what additional tradeoff follows?', ['Higher output support comes with additional price pressure','Output support also necessarily restores the original low price level','Demand expansion shifts SRAS back directly with no price effect','Demand expansion lowers output further while reducing prices'], 'Expansion moves along the worse supply relation. It can support output but does not remove the higher production costs.','Treats demand support as a direct reversal of a supply shock.')
bridge('ECON-SP-MONEY-DEMAND-INTEREST-RATE-6030','Income rises and money demand increases, with money supply fixed. If investment is interest-sensitive, what follow-on pressure results?', ['Higher interest reduces investment','Lower interest raises investment','Higher interest raises investment','Investment is unchanged by assumption whenever income changes'], 'Greater money demand raises the equilibrium rate; the higher financing cost reduces interest-sensitive investment.','Reverses the interest-rate-to-investment link.')
bridge('LG-B-6017','Money demand rises at fixed supply. A firm borrows at the resulting market interest rate. Relative to before the shift, what happens to its financing cost?', ['It rises as the equilibrium interest rate rises','It falls as the equilibrium interest rate rises','It falls because demand for money means demand for bonds rises','It stays fixed because money demand cannot affect rates'], 'With a fixed quantity of money, higher demand raises its opportunity cost, the interest rate faced by borrowers.','Confuses demand for money with demand for interest-bearing bonds.')
bridge('ECON-SP-MONETARY-POLICY-TRANSMISSION-6031','In the scarce-reserves textbook model, monetary expansion lowers interest but firms do not increase investment. Which next part of the usual transmission chain is weakened?', ['The investment-driven rise in aggregate demand','The already-observed fall in interest','The definition of required reserves','The classification of currency in money'], 'The chain from rates to investment fails to deliver the usual spending increase, so the investment contribution to AD is weaker.','Assumes an observed rate cut guarantees a spending increase.')
bridge('LG-B-6019','In a scarce-reserves textbook model, monetary expansion lowers rates and investment rises$10. With spending multiplier2 and no other spending change, what AD effect follows?', ['A$20 increase','A$10 decrease','A$5 increase','A$20 decrease'], 'The autonomous investment increase10 is multiplied by2, raising AD20.','Reverses the direction of expansionary monetary transmission.',('10*2',20))
bridge('LG-B-6020','In a scarce-reserves textbook model, monetary contraction raises rates and reduces investment$15. With spending multiplier2 and no other spending change, what AD effect follows?', ['A$30 decrease','A$15 increase','A$7.5 decrease','A$30 increase'], 'The autonomous investment reduction15 produces a total demand reduction30.','Reverses the direction of contractionary monetary transmission.',('15*2',30))
bridge('ECON-SP-FISCAL-POLICY-AD-SHIFT-6032','The government reduces purchases$20 during a recession. With spending multiplier2 and no offsets, how does this affect the existing demand shortfall?', ['It widens the shortfall by$40','It narrows the shortfall by$40','It widens the shortfall by$10','It has no effect because purchases are transfers'], 'The negative purchases impulse is multiplied:−20×2=−40. It reinforces a demand recession.','Treats a purchases cut as expansionary demand policy.',('-20*2',-40))
bridge('ECON-SP-TAX-CUT-AD-6033','A$100 tax cut raises disposable income. With MPC=.8, how much first-round consumption is added before induced spending rounds?', ['$80','$100','$500','$20'], 'First-round consumption=MPC×tax cut=.8×100=80. This is not yet the total multiplier effect.','Treats the full tax cut as first-round consumption.',('.8*100',80))
bridge('LG-B-6021','A tax cut produces an$80 first-round consumption increase. MPC=.75 with no other leakages or offsets. What total AD increase follows after induced rounds?', ['$320','$80','$60','$240'], 'The spending multiplier is1/(1−.75)=4; multiply the already-specified first-round impulse80 by4 to get320.','Applies MPC again to an impulse already stated as consumption.',('80/(1-.75)',320))
bridge('ECON-SP-CALCULATE-SPENDING-MULTIPLIER-6035','With MPC=.8 and no offsets, what purchases increase is needed for a$100 total AD increase?', ['$20','$80','$100','$500'], 'Multiplier=5, so required autonomous purchases=100/5=20.','Multiplies the target by the multiplier instead of dividing.',('100*(1-.8)',20))
bridge('ECON-SP-CROWDING-OUT-EFFECT-6036','Fiscal expansion would add$100 to AD before crowding out. Higher rates reduce AD by a total$30 after induced investment effects. What net increase remains?', ['$70','$130','$30','$100'], 'Subtract the already-total offset once:100−30=70.','Adds crowding out to demand instead of subtracting it.',('100-30',70))
bridge('LG-B-6023','A demand gap is$250. Fiscal policy adds$300 gross to AD, while displaced investment reduces AD by a total$80 including induced effects. How much of the original gap remains in this fixed-price planning model?', ['$30 remains','$50 of excess demand appears','$80 remains','$220 remains'], 'Net impulse=300−80=220; remaining gap=250−220=30.','Uses the gross fiscal impulse instead of its net effect on the gap.',('250-(300-80)',30))
bridge('ECON-SP-LIQUIDITY-TRAP-6038','Rates are near zero and additional money barely changes investment. Officials propose another identical monetary expansion solely to stimulate investment. Which forecast follows from the stated constraint?', ['A limited additional investment response','A guaranteed large response because every reserve dollar becomes investment','A guaranteed rise in interest and fall in money supply','A direct improvement in technology independent of spending'], 'The weak rate/spending channel limits the same instrument’s extra demand effect in the stated near-zero-rate setting.','Treats monetary expansion as a guaranteed large investment stimulus despite a binding transmission limit.')
bridge('ECON-SP-POLICY-LAGS-6037','A stimulus approved during recession arrives after output has returned to potential. With no new negative shock, which risk follows from the implementation lag?', ['It can push demand above capacity','It automatically reverses direction and contracts demand','It is costless because the initial recession justified it','It lowers potential output by changing the approval date'], 'The demand addition arrives after its original gap has closed and can create excess demand.','Assesses policy only at approval rather than implementation.')

bridge('ECON-SP-MOVE-ALONG-SRPC-6041','After a demand boom lowers unemployment, expected inflation rises. At the same unemployment rate, how does the new SRPC’s inflation compare with the old one?', ['Higher','Lower','Unchanged','Exactly zero'], 'Higher expected inflation shifts SRPC upward, raising inflation at a given unemployment rate.','Treats changed expectations as a movement along the original SRPC.')
bridge('ECON-SP-NATURAL-RATE-HYPOTHESIS-6043','A demand surprise briefly reduces unemployment from5% to4%. Natural unemployment is unchanged. Once expectations fully adjust, what rate is the long-run benchmark?', ['5%','4%','1%','0%'], 'With no change in natural unemployment, the surprise cannot permanently sustain4%; the long-run benchmark remains5%.','Treats a temporary demand-driven unemployment gain as a lower natural rate.')
bridge('ECON-SP-READ-PC-AXES-6040','A contraction lowers inflation from6% to4% along a fixed SRPC. In the short run, what unemployment change should accompany that disinflation?', ['Unemployment rises','Unemployment falls','Unemployment must stay fixed','Natural unemployment automatically falls2 points'], 'A movement down a fixed downward-sloping SRPC entails higher unemployment as inflation falls. It does not itself change the natural rate.','Claims disinflation along a fixed SRPC has no unemployment cost.')
bridge('ECON-SP-IDENTIFY-LRPC-6042','Demand expansion raises inflation persistently, and expectations eventually catch up. If natural unemployment is5%, which long-run pair is possible?', ['Higher inflation with unemployment back at5%','Higher inflation with unemployment permanently below5% solely from the surprise','Lower inflation with unemployment permanently above5% solely from the surprise','Unchanged inflation with unemployment fixed at zero'], 'Full adjustment eliminates the initial unemployment surprise. The long-run rate stays natural while demand policy can leave higher inflation.','Infers a permanent unemployment gain from persistent demand inflation.')
bridge('ECON-SP-TRACE-EXPECTATIONS-ADJUSTMENT-6045','Two economies have the same natural unemployment rate but settle at different fully expected inflation rates. What does the vertical LRPC imply about their long-run unemployment rates?', ['They can be equal despite different inflation rates','The higher-inflation economy must have lower unemployment','The higher-inflation economy must have higher unemployment','Neither can be at its natural rate when inflation is positive'], 'A vertical long-run curve permits different inflation rates at the same natural unemployment rate once expectations adjust.','Extends the short-run tradeoff to fully expected long-run inflation.')
bridge('ECON-SP-SUPPLY-SHOCK-PC-SHIFT-6046','A disinflation target becomes more credible, lowering expected inflation before demand is reduced further. What change in the short-run Phillips relation can ease reaching the target?', ['A downward SRPC shift allows lower inflation at a given unemployment rate','An upward SRPC shift allows lower inflation at a given unemployment rate','A rightward LRPC shift automatically removes all output cost','No relation can change until unemployment reaches zero'], 'Lower expected inflation shifts SRPC down. That can reduce the needed demand contraction, without guaranteeing a completely costless transition.','Reverses the expectations shift or equates credibility with a lower natural rate.')
bridge('PM2A-LRPC-BR-021','Job matching permanently improves and natural unemployment falls from5% to4%. What happens to the LRPC?', ['It shifts left to4% unemployment','It shifts right to6% unemployment','It remains at5% and only actual inflation changes','It becomes horizontal at4% inflation'], 'LRPC is located at natural unemployment. A lower natural rate shifts the vertical benchmark left.','Treats a changed natural rate as movement along a fixed LRPC.')
bridge('PM2A-LRPC-BR-022','A disinflation temporarily pushes output below potential. Inflation falls3 percentage points and cumulative output loss is6% of annual output. What sacrifice ratio describes that transition?', ['2','0.5','3','18'], 'Ratio=6/3=2 percent of annual output sacrificed per percentage-point inflation reduction.','Reverses the sacrifice-ratio numerator and denominator.',('6/3',2))
bridge('ECON-SP-CALCULATE-SACRIFICE-RATIO-6050','A disinflation’s annual output losses are1% and3% of one year’s output. Inflation falls2 percentage points. What is the sacrifice ratio?', ['2','0.5','1.5','4'], 'Add losses1+3=4 and divide by the2-point inflation reduction:4/2=2.','Averages annual losses instead of summing cumulative output loss.',('(1+3)/2',2))
bridge('ECON-SP-INTERPRET-SACRIFICE-RATIO-6051','Two plans each reduce inflation4 percentage points. Plan A has sacrifice ratio2; Plan B has ratio1.5. Which has the lower estimated cumulative output loss?', ['B:6% versus A:8% of annual output','A:2% versus B:2.67% of annual output','Both:4% because the inflation targets match','B:1.5% versus A:2% regardless of the size of disinflation'], 'Loss=ratio×inflation reduction. A:2×4=8;B:1.5×4=6.','Compares ratio levels without scaling by the inflation reduction.',('1.5*4',6),('2*4',8))
bridge('PM2A-DIS-BR-021','A policy’s estimated sacrifice ratio is2.5. If it aims to reduce inflation2 percentage points, what cumulative output loss should the planning comparison use?', ['5% of annual output','1.25% of annual output','2.5% of annual output','0.8% of annual output'], 'Multiply the cost per inflation point by the2-point target:2.5×2=5%.','Divides by the inflation target when converting a ratio into total loss.',('2.5*2',5))
bridge('PMOE-NER-BR-001','Quote e as foreign units per domestic unit and define the real exchange rate as eP/P*. The nominal rate rises10%, while domestic and foreign prices are unchanged. Does the nominal appreciation also represent real appreciation?', ['Yes; the real rate rises10%','No; the real rate falls10%','No; the real rate stays unchanged whenever prices are unchanged','Yes; but the real rate rises20% because both price levels enter'], 'With P/P* unchanged, the real rate changes in the same proportion as e. Under this quotation the rise is appreciation.','Assumes a nominal change cannot affect the real rate when price levels stay fixed.')

